<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madra Kingdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> were an ancient people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, named in Vedic texts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and Buddhist sources, with their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a>, modern Sialkot. In the epic, Madri, the wife of Pandu, and her brother Shalya are Madras. Buddhist tradition records that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara of Magadha</a> took a Madra princess, Khema, as one of his wives.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition gives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> more than one founder. The Vayu Purana names King Ushinara Shibi of the Anu line, Anu being a son of Yayati, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> names Madra, a son of the Anu king Shibi. A myth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> gives the Madra and Salwa tribes a common origin in the seven sons of Bhadra, wife of the Puru king Vyushitaswa: three became kings of Salwa and four kings of Madra. Besides the kingdom with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a>, sometimes called Eastern Madra, a Western and a Northern Madra are believed to have existed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the epic, Bhishma obtained Madri as a bride for Pandu by paying wealth to her kinsmen, as Madra custom required, and her sons were the Pandava twins Nakula and Sahadeva. Nakula later came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> on his western campaign and received jewels and gems from Shalya. Though Shalya set out with his army to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a>, Duryodhana's men received and paid his troops on the way, and he fought for the Kauravas, drove Karna's chariot for a day and, as their commander on the last day of the Kurukshetra War, was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a>.</p><p>Outside the epic, the Allahabad Pillar inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> (r. c. 350–375 CE) names the Madrakas among the frontier peoples who paid him tribute, obeyed his orders and came to his court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The epic counts Madra among the Bahlika or western countries, which it describes as cold lands where people used blankets, reared sheep and drank their milk, knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and bred horses of excellent quality. Their horses and horsemen served in the wars of the kings of Aryavarta, and their soldiers are said to have fought for either side for payment. These western lands were also called Arashtra or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratta">Aratta</a>, a kingless country, which raises the question whether they were republics ruled by elected chiefs. Some passages place the Madrakas among the tribes beyond the kingdoms of Aryavarta.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madra kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He strengthened his position through marriages with the royal houses of Kosala, which brought him a village of Kashi as dowry, of the Licchavis of Vaishali and of the <b>Madras of the Punjab</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
